Z||000005||Haze_Performance||02-28-2001||03:22 PM||||Im have:<P>On the 85- 17"x7.5" with a 40mm offset all around.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FronthouseDside2.jpg"> <P>On the 87- 16"x7.5" with a 38mm offset all around.<P><BR>I went to the shop today....Im thinking of changing the 85' to 18"x7.5" (front) 18"x9" (rear, w/coilovers)Hmmmmmmm....<P><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ttttttt.gif">  </A>       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>PFF Members Photo Page</A><BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1431213" TARGET=_blank><BR>Photopoint Gallery (Updated Weekly)</A>||||reg||10
Z||000006||87sportcoupe||02-28-2001||03:28 PM||||When the tire says 215/40-17<BR>Is 40 the offset... or the skinniness of the tire?<BR>Do you need different tires for 17x7 and 17x7.5   or would 215/40-17 work for both<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000007||stimpy||02-28-2001||03:36 PM||||Offset has nothing to do with the tire. That "40" in the tire size is the aspect ratio, or "profile".  That means that the sidewall height is only 40% of the width of the tire.  The offset measurement is how far from the centerline of the tire that the hub acually bolts on to the wheel.||||reg||1
